  "query_preview": "..A few showers/storms have begun firing up across the\nnorthern portion of our CWA. With storm motion being 20-30 kts, \nflash flooding concerns are lower, but can't completely rule it \nout if we start to see storms back-building. This more isolated \nstorm coverage looks to continue through tomorrow afternoon, but \nthen we'll gradually see greater precipitation coverage heading \ninto the weekend and early next week. \n\nThis moisture surge will primarily be due to Tropical Storm Marie\nbuilding south of the California Baja coast. Between this \ntropical system and a large trough descending from the Pacific \nNorthwest, the synoptic pattern will favor a more saturated \nsouthwesterly flow aloft. Simultaneously, high pressure looks to \nstrengthen across the Midwest and also push some additional \nmoisture into the state. Due to a few different factors \ncontributing to the potential active weekend/early next week, our \nconfidence is still moderate at this time. Certainly higher \nconfidence in the increase in storm/shower potential, but the \ntiming and specific locations are still up in the air. For now, \nthe greatest chances for activity look to be across the higher \nterrain, especially across the Kaibab Plateau and White Mountains.\n\nDaily chances for activity look to stay in the forecast for the\nforeseeable future. As Tropical Storm Marie moves further \nwestward in the Pacific (by early to mid next week), the high \npressure previously mentioned is projected to shift westward. \nWhere models start to diverge is where the high eventually \nsettles. There's still time to see how things shake out in the\nlong-range forecast, but for now expect additional showers/storms\nFriday onward. \n\nOther than precipitation, expect breezy winds through the end of\nthe week, along with daytime high temperatures generally below\nnormal for this time of year.",
